---
description: Use before adding a new abstraction, dependency, layer, or design pattern in app/, server/, or shared/. Do not use for an ordinary component or composable decomposition with no new abstraction — see architecture-refactoring.mdc for that.
globs:
  - "app/**/*"
  - "server/**/*"
  - "shared/**/*"
alwaysApply: false
---

- Before adding an abstraction, layer, dependency, or design pattern, answer
  four questions: what problem it solves, why the obvious simpler alternative
  does not, what it makes harder, and what breaks if the codebase or its usage
  grows several times over. If you cannot answer all four, the abstraction is
  not ready.
- A design pattern is a named solution to a recurring change problem; adopt one
  only when that change is real and repeated, never to give code a familiar
  shape. A `switch`/`if` you touch rarely is fine; a strategy object with one
  strategy is not.
- Judge a new dependency between modules by three factors: how strongly they
  are coupled (a shared contract or type is cheaper than shared internals), how
  far apart they are (a cross-module edge costs more than a same-file one), and
  how often the dependency changes. Avoid combining high strength, high
  distance, and high volatility on the same edge; `server/integrations/<vendor>/`
  exists precisely to isolate a volatile external dependency behind a stable
  contract.
- Promote a helper, type, or component to a shared module on its second real,
  independent use, not in anticipation of one.
- This rule decides *whether* to add an abstraction; see
  `.cursor/rules/architecture-refactoring.mdc` for the accompanying
  decomposition mechanics and the "Architecture boundaries" invariant in
  `AGENTS.md` for the inward-dependency rule this all serves.
